//     summary: "Desperate for children after learning of Kunti's sons, Gandhari seeks a blessing from Sage Vyasa but delivers a lifeless mass of flesh. Vyasa splits the mass into 100 pots, which eventually incubate and birth the 100 Kaurava brothers, led by Duryodhana. His arrival signals a dark future for the blind King Dhritarashtra's lineage.",
//     deepLore: `Upon learning about the children born to Kunti and Madri, Gandhari felt an overwhelming sense of desperation. Despite the passage of many years, she had not conceived. In her moments of despair, sage Vyasa arrived. Gandhari welcomed him with open arms, and her devotion impressed the sage. He asked her to name her reward. "I wish for a hundred strong sons," she expressed. Vyasa promised her, and shortly after, she fell pregnant.\n\nBut two years passed without the arrival of her children. Frustrated, Gandhari repeatedly struck her own belly. The impact triggered labor, but to her horror, she gave birth not to a child, but to a solid mass of flesh resembling an iron ball. Heartbroken, she commanded her attendants to dispose of it. At that moment, Vyasa reappeared and instructed, "Stop. I will prepare what needs to be done." \n\nHe created 100 pots, carefully breaking the mass of flesh into equal portions and placing each piece into a separate jar. During this process, Gandhari silently wished for a daughter as well. Seeing her thoughts, Vyasa assured her, "The last bit of flesh will produce your daughter." \n\nEventually, the firstborn son emerged from one of the pots, wailing as his lungs filled with air. The wild animals outside Hastinapur responded with cries, the winds howled violently, and fierce fires broke out around the kingdom. This firstborn son was named Duryodhana. One by one, his 99 brothers followed suit, emerging from their jars until a daughter named Dushala was born. These children came to be known as the Kauravas. \n\nDhritarashtra felt immense gratitude to finally have heirs. Yet, he was acutely aware that Yudhisthira, the son of King Pandu, would inherit the throne of Hastinapur as the eldest of the generation. This realization bred jealousy and resentment in Dhritarashtra's heart, setting the stage for a fierce rivalry.`,
