//     summary: "The mysterious archer is revealed to be Drona, a master warrior seeking vengeance against his former friend, King Drupada. Hired as the royal instructor, Drona hones the princes' skills, with Arjuna emerging as his unparalleled star pupil. As his teaching fee, Drona commands his students to capture Panchala's king, solidifying the princes' martial dominance.",
//     deepLore: `Upon hearing about the princes' encounter with the mysterious archer, Bhishma immediately recognized that this must be the legendary Drona. Drona had just returned from a disheartening meeting with King Drupada, which had left him feeling humiliated. In his youth, Drona had been close friends with Drupada. Drupada had promised Drona that when he ascended to the throne, he would share his kingdom with him. \n\nYears passed, and Drona struggled to provide for his family. Desperate, he visited King Drupada, reminding him of his childhood promise. When he approached the king, Drupada laughingly dismissed Drona's plea. "Kings cannot be friends with those who are poor and powerless," he proclaimed. The court echoed his laughter, leaving Drona humiliated, embittered, and furious. \n\nAs he left the palace, he stumbled upon the princes attempting to retrieve their ball. Bhishma recognized Drona's talent and appointed him as the instructor for the princes. Drona dedicated himself wholeheartedly to training them. Among the students, Bhima and Duryodhana displayed remarkable prowess with the mace, while Yudhisthira excelled with the spear. Nakula and Sahadeva proved their skill with the swords. However, it was Arjuna who captured Drona's admiration, quickly becoming his favorite student. \n\nOne day, Drona set up a wooden bird in a tree and instructed the princes to aim for its eye. He asked each prince what they saw. Yudhisthira responded, "I see the bird, the branch, and the tree." "No good," replied Drona. When it was Arjuna's turn, he replied confidently, "I see only the eye." He let his arrow fly, striking the eye dead on. \n\nAs the princes honed their skills, Drona soon requested his Guru Dakshina—the traditional fee paid by a student. "Bring me King Drupada," Drona commanded. The princes set off, quickly overcame Drupada's guards in Panchala, and brought him to Drona's feet. Drona said, "Once you claimed only a king can befriend another king. I sought your kingdom so I could earn your friendship, and I have succeeded. But out of mercy, I return half to you." Drupada accepted with a tight smile, but the humiliation would linger, and the two former friends would one day find themselves on opposing sides of a great war.`,
